families.entry_rule:
At the close of t, using RuleFeatures fields.
Market conditions:
- systemic_t (share_hit2_t >= 0.10);
- not half_day_t;
- M_pct_t >= theta.
Name conditions:
- eligible, price_ok (raw close >= $5) and liquid20 (median dollar volume over t-20..t-1 >= $20M);
- x_t >= 2: it was hit by the flush, with a drawdown-to-low of 2 own sigma or more;
- v_t <= -1: its close-to-close beta residual u = (r - beta*rB)/sigma is at least 1 own sigma below its segment's median that day;
- g_seg_t > -2: its own overnight gap is not 2 sigma or more below its segment's. A larger gap points to its own news, such as earnings landing in the selloff.
Rank by v ascending, keep at most 5 new names per day (cap=5, priority='v'), and allow no re-entry while held (registry.entries).

families.options_expression:
Instrument per name at the close of t, from the live Robinhood chain (broker IV and delta).
Contract: the put nearest delta -0.35 (accept -0.25 to -0.45). Expiry: the first listed expiry at least H sessions after the entry session and at most H+5. That is about 7-14 calendar days for H = 5 and 14-21 for H = 10. Names without such an expiry trade shares.
Filters (otherwise shares):
- quoted spread <= 10% of mid;
- open interest >= 250;
- mid >= $0.30;
- 100 x strike no larger than the name's allocation;
- no earnings date before expiry;
- put IV no more than 5 vol points above the same strike-and-expiry call. Expensive puts signal borrow stress or informed selling (Ofek, Richardson and Whitelaw 2004; Cremers and Weinbaum 2010). When the call's IV exceeds the put's by 2 points or more, use a buy-write at the same strike instead (the same payoff, selling the richer leg).

Richness: rho = IV*sqrt(D/365) / (MIV*sqrt(h/252)), where D is calendar days and h sessions to expiry, and MIV = sigma_t*sqrt(252)*sqrt((1/h)*sum_{j=1..h} sum_k [pi_t A^j]_k s_k^2). Here pi_t = (P_calm, P_stressed, P_panic) at t, and A and s come from the refit in force (frozen v3 live). The backtest needs each refit's full A saved; pit_refits.json keeps only its diagonal, and using frozen v3's A would leak 2026 into the yardstick.
Choice: CSP if rho >= lambda_S; long call (delta nearest +0.60, same expiry rule, dollar delta near the allocation, premium <= 30% of it) if rho <= lambda_C; otherwise shares. The call branch should be rare, because IV is high after flushes.

Judging without an IV surface, after the equity gate:
(1) Price-only replay on the chosen variant's dev events. For lambda from 0.8 to 2.0 in steps of 0.05, set IV = lambda x MIV and choose the strike by delta at that IV (options.strike_for_delta, RATE 4%, q = 0). Take the payoff from the actual close at expiry, plus the next-open sale if assigned. Charge 4% of premium per traded side (10% as the 2.5x stress). This yields lambda_0, where the CSP breaks even; lambda_S, where the CSP's mean return on cash equals the share version's on the same events, net of costs; and lambda_C, below which the call beats shares.
(2) Draw 12 events, seeded and stratified by episode: from dev if Robinhood still serves the expired contracts, else from 2026 events after the holdout read. Fetch one contract each: the listed strike nearest the rule's strike at IV = lambda_S x MIV. Back the actual IV out of its entry-day bar (options.implied_vol on the mean of open and close, against the underlying's mean of open and close; about 2-3 vol points of error) to get rho_actual.
(3) Use CSPs only if the median rho_actual >= lambda_S and at least 8 of the 12 have rho_actual >= lambda_0; otherwise trade shares. Report real paired option-minus-share P/L as a sanity check, not a test.
(4) Record both expressions on paper for every live ticket.

Options beat shares when IV is rich and the rebound is modest next to the premium yield: a 1-2 week 35-delta put in a stressed state pays roughly 1.5-4% of strike, and the premium cushions a second leg.
They are worse:
- when the edge comes from sharp V-rebounds, which the CSP caps at the premium. Pre-declared check: if the top decile of events gives more than 60% of the share version's total excess, use shares;
- when IV has already collapsed by the t+1 open;
- under the one-day delay, because the premium decays fast after the spike;
- in illiquid small caps;
- in high-priced names, where one contract exceeds the allocation.

families.entry_rule:
At the close of t, using RuleFeatures fields.
Market conditions: not systemic_t (share_hit2_t < 0.10, a calm-market day) and not half_day_t.
Name conditions:
- eligible, price_ok and liquid20;
- x_t >= 3: a drawdown-to-low of 3 own sigma or more;
- v_t <= -2: it closed at least 2 own sigma below its segment's median beta residual, so the loss is stock-specific and did not recover by the close;
- g_seg_t > -1: no stock-specific overnight gap of 1 sigma or more. The damage happened during the session, so it is unlikely to be a scheduled after-hours release.
Rank by v ascending, cap 5 new names per day, no re-entry while held.

families.options_expression:
The same rules as A: the put nearest delta -0.35, the first expiry at least H and at most H+5 sessions after entry, the same liquidity, earnings and parity filters, rho measured against MIV. CSP if rho >= lambda_S, long call if rho <= lambda_C, otherwise shares, with the lambda thresholds from C's own price-only replay.
Specific to C:
(a) After idiosyncratic hits the S1 model over-forecasts persistence (F5), so MIV is biased up and rho biased down for these names. C's replay uses the same MIV, so its thresholds absorb that bias. A fixed threshold would not, and would push C wrongly toward calls.
(b) Real-contract check: 12 seeded events from the shared 30-contract budget, with the same decision rule as A (median rho_actual >= lambda_S, and at least 8 of 12 >= lambda_0).
Options are worse than shares:
- when post-shock IV is not elevated (the single-stock variance premium is small, and IV may track realized volatility closely);
- when the stock rebounds fast and far (the CSP is capped);
- when an unscheduled news shock keeps going (the CSP's long delta loses like shares, less the premium);
- in the mid caps that dominate C's events, where option spreads are wider.

families.mechanism:
Bad news released as a discrete, stock-specific, after-hours announcement (earnings, guidance) can be incorporated slowly:
- post-earnings drift (Bernard and Thomas 1989, 1990);
- news-driven continuation (Chan 2003; Savor 2012);
- slow diffusion of bad news under short-sale constraints and thin coverage (Hong, Lim and Stein 2000; Diamond and Verrecchia 1987);
- investor inattention (Hirshleifer, Lim and Teoh 2009; DellaVigna and Pollet 2009).
The account cannot short, so long puts are the only expression.
Evidence against:
- drift has largely disappeared in large caps (Martineau 2022; Chordia, Subrahmanyam and Tong 2014);
- discrete jumps draw full attention and little drift (Da, Gurun and Warachka 2014);
- long puts carry the variance premium and have negative expected returns in general (Coval and Shumway 2001; Bakshi and Kapadia 2003).
Its main value is as the news side of the shock-type x information split, the contrast for C's signature.

families.entry_rule:
At the close of t, using RuleFeatures fields.
Market conditions: not systemic_t and not half_day_t.
Name conditions:
- eligible, price_ok and liquid20;
- x_t >= 3;
- v_t <= -2;
- g_seg_t <= -2: a stock-specific overnight gap of 2 own sigma or more, meaning information released outside market hours, typically earnings or guidance.
Rank by v ascending, cap 5 new names per day, no re-entry while held.
